# NIZAM - environment template for the `scheduler` service. TEMPLATE ONLY. NOTHING HERE IS EXECUTED.
#
# Owning contract: PFOS 12 - Two-Agent VPS Deployment & Operations, §3.2.2 (the scheduler is one of
#   only two services permitted a cross-store view, and read-only at that - it takes none), §3.2.7
#   (one environment file per service), §8.2 (the coarse halt is honoured here too), §7.6 (this
#   path).
# Spec: .kiro/specs/06-two-agent-vps/ - task 7.3. Contract 12, phase 7. Requirements R6, R23, R24.
# Steering: .kiro/steering/two-agent-vps.md §0b, §2 (writing this file is permitted, running it is
#   not), §7 (placeholders only, AC09).
#
# Companion: ops/docker-compose.yml declares this file as this service's single env_file, under the
#   placeholder <SCHEDULER_ENV_PATH>, attaches this service to the internal tick network only, and
#   mounts NO store into it. Audited by src/server/ops/envTemplates.ts.
#
# HOW TO READ AN ENTRY. Each assignment is preceded by exactly three annotation lines:
#   what:    one line saying what the value is
#   gate:    which human gate in ops/GATE_REGISTER.md supplies it - G1..G8, or `operator` for a
#            value the operator simply chooses, or `build` for a path to an artefact the build emits
#   secret:  yes if disclosing the value would let someone reach or impersonate this deployment
#
# EVERY VALUE IS AN <ANGLE_BRACKET> PLACEHOLDER WHOSE NAME IS ITS OWN ENTRY NAME.
#
# WHAT THIS SERVICE IS. A clock, and nothing else. It delivers a tick to each agent over an internal
# network and the agent decides what a tick means. It reads no store, holds no credential, and makes
# no outbound call, which is why this file is four entries long and two of them are the halt.

# what: the internal address the life agent accepts a tick on
# gate: operator
# secret: no
LIFE_TICK_ENDPOINT=<LIFE_TICK_ENDPOINT>

# what: the internal address the finance agent accepts a tick on
# gate: operator
# secret: no
FINANCE_TICK_ENDPOINT=<FINANCE_TICK_ENDPOINT>

# what: how often a tick is delivered
# gate: operator
# secret: no
SCHEDULER_TICK_INTERVAL=<SCHEDULER_TICK_INTERVAL>

# ---- the kill switch, both forms (contract 12 §8) ---------------------------------------------

# what: the sentinel path checked per tick; present means stop, and it is never cached
# gate: G1
# secret: no
KILL_SENTINEL_PATH=<KILL_SENTINEL_PATH>

# what: the coarse halt; 1 halts the scheduler, 0 does not. Restart-scoped, so not a panic stop
# gate: operator
# secret: no
NIZAM_KILL_ALL=<NIZAM_KILL_ALL>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately absent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. NO STORE PATH. Contract 12 §3.2.2 permits the scheduler a read-only cross-store mount, and it
#    takes none, because it has no read-only work to do in a store: a tick is a signal to the agent
#    that owns the store, not a query against it. ops/docker-compose.yml mounts no store here, so a
#    path entry would name something absent from this namespace.
#
# 2. NO CREDENTIAL, AND NO MODEL KEY. A clock that could authenticate could also be made to act. The
#    tick endpoints are internal-network addresses; reaching them requires already being on that
#    network, which nothing off-host is.
#
# 3. NO WEEKLY CAP. Steering §4 invariant 6 puts the cap on the two agents, because they are what
#    spends. The scheduler causes work; it does not pay for it, and a cap here would be a second
#    ceiling that could disagree with the one the ledger actually enforces.
#
# 4. NO CONSENT BUS ENDPOINT. ops/BUS_NETWORK_BINDING.md item 4 makes the two agents the bus's only
#    clients, and ops/docker-compose.yml deliberately leaves this service off the bus network. An
#    endpoint entry here would be a client the binding requirement does not permit.
#
# 5. NO ACCESS TO THE HALT IT OBSERVES. The sentinel volume is mounted read-only, like it is in every
#    other consumer, so this service can see the halt and cannot clear it. Only the operator does
#    (§8, final rule).
